From: reelee81 <reelee81@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 19:02:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 9/18] fitz: Decode only the needed area of a JPX image.

Drawing a zoomed part of a JPEG 2000 image always decoded the whole
image, because the JPX branch of compressed_image_get_pixmap() could not
return a subarea. Each zoomed tile of a 10200x14031 image needed about
2 GB and several seconds.

The JPX branch now asks OpenJPEG to decode only the requested area
(opj_set_decode_area), at the same reduced resolution as before. The
area is widened so that it starts on a sample of every component and on
the grid of the l2factor; an image whose origin is not on that grid is
decoded whole as before, and so is an image whose area fails to decode.

So that tiles drawn from areas of an image match each other and the
whole image:
- the 90% rule of the image decode tuning is not applied to JPX images,
  so a partly visible image is drawn from an area;
- an area is placed on the pixel grid of the whole image, which spreads
  its pixels evenly over the image size, so an area and the whole image
  have the same geometry, also when the area comes from the store;
- an area that still needs subsampling averages exactly the blocks that
  fz_subsample_pixmap() uses for the whole image; the area is widened by
  one block to the left and top for this;
- an area is not snapped to whole pixels again when it is scaled,
  because its edges lie inside the image.

The OpenJPEG stream skip callback now returns the number of bytes
skipped instead of the new position. Decoding an area skips the unused
tiles, which failed with "Stream too short" once a skip went past the
stream buffer.

Without an area request the output is unchanged. It needs 0010 for an
exact area decode of 9/7 images.

Modified for ReePDF on 2026-11-11.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
